---
import Card from "astro-theme-university/components/Card.astro";
import CardGrid from "astro-theme-university/components/CardGrid.astro";
import ContentLayout from "astro-theme-university/layouts/ContentLayout.astro";
import heroImage from "../assets/images/hero-home.avif";
import { courseMeta } from "../course-config";
import { sessionLabels, siteConfig } from "../site-config";

---

<ContentLayout
  title={`${courseMeta.code}: ${courseMeta.title}`}
  description={courseMeta.description}
  heroImage={heroImage}
  heroImageAlt="A cross-section of layered ground in gold and black on warm cream, dense bands at the base thinning upward into broken dashes, with a single fine line cutting down through every layer, in a two-ink risograph print"
  {...siteConfig}
>
  <ul class="course-tags" aria-label="Course tags">
    {courseMeta.tags.map((tag) => <li>{tag}</li>)}
  </ul>

  <h2>What you will do</h2>
  <p>
    Pick a dead codebase — someone's abandoned project, its last commit
    gathering dust — and spend a semester working out what it was, how it
    worked, why it died, and whether it deserves to come back.
  </p>
  <p>
    You will learn forensic techniques that no other course teaches: reading
    <code>git blame</code> the way a geologist reads rock strata, dating a
    project by its dependency manifest, reconstructing a team's working culture
    from the messages they left behind. By week 8 you will know your dig site
    better than its original authors did. By week 12 you will present it to an
    audience who has never seen it, and your job is to make them care.
  </p>

  <h2>Who it is for</h2>
  <p>
    Postgraduate students who have already shipped software and inherited
    somebody else's. The course assumes you can read an unfamiliar codebase in
    at least one language and work independently for twelve weeks on a single
    object of study; it assumes no archaeology, no historiography and no prior
    exposure to conservation theory, all of which are taught here.
  </p>
  <p>
    It is an 8000-level course because the work is interpretive rather than
    procedural. There is no correct answer to what killed a project, only a
    reading you can evidence and defend — and the assessment is weighted
    accordingly, toward argument rather than output.
  </p>

  <h2>How it is taught</h2>
  <p>
    Each week pairs a lecture with a seminar. The lecture supplies the method
    and its literature — the course borrows openly from archaeological
    stratigraphy, from conservation ethics, and from oral-history practice,
    because each has spent a century on problems software has spent twenty
    years rediscovering. The seminar is where you apply that week's method to
    your own dig site and defend the result to the room.
  </p>

  <h2>Where to go next</h2>
  <CardGrid columns={2}>
    <Card title={sessionLabels.plural} href="/sessions/">
      <p>Twelve weeks, twelve forensic methods, one dig site of your choosing.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="Lectures" href="/lectures/">
      <p>The method behind each week, and the real projects it was learned on.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="Assessment" href="/assessments/">
      <p>Three pieces: a survey, a diagnosis, and an exhibit.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="People" href="/people/">
      <p>Who teaches the course and how to reach them.</p>
    </Card>
  </CardGrid>
</ContentLayout>

<style>
  .course-tags {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    margin-block: 0 2rem;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  .course-tags li {
    padding: 0.25rem 0.65rem;
    border-radius: 999px;
    color: var(--at-text-secondary);
    background: var(--at-bg-alt);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
  }
</style>
